{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
## The runtime

This build runs on no engine, so the layer beneath the game is yours as well,
rendering the 3D yard on the installed `three` included. `specs/overview.md`
states what Gantry needs from that layer and fixes nothing else about it.
{{else}}
## The {{engine.name}} engine

This build runs on the **{{engine.name}}** engine, installed as a dependency of
the project, so import it by its bare package name; there is nothing to add to
`package.json` for it and nothing to install.

The engine's own documentation is at `{{engine.docs}}`. It is the authoritative
description of the engine's API; the specification in `specs/` describes the
game and does not restate the engine.
{{/if}}

## What to read first

The full specification is in the `specs/` directory at `{{workspace}}`, already
present in your project. Produce an implementation that matches the
specification exactly. `specs/overview.md` states what you implement, which
files stay as they are, the code quality the build is held to, and the commands
that run over the finished repository. `specs/assets.md` states the models and
sound you produce with the asset tools on this machine's `PATH` and commit with
the build.

## The build interface

`npm ci` then `npm run build` must produce the static site, with no other manual
step, into `dist/` at the project root, with an `index.html` at the root of that
directory. That directory must run when served as-is from any base path, at the
root of a static file server or mounted under a sub-path of one. The scripts
that do this are already in `package.json`.

## Driving the game in a browser

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
The project's Playwright and its headless Chromium are installed, so you can
serve the built game and drive it in a real browser, click a crane together,
check for console errors, or exercise `window.__gantry`.
{{else}}
The project's Playwright and its headless Chromium are installed, so you can
serve the built game and drive it in a real browser, click a crane together, and
check for console errors.
{{/if}}

When you launch the browser, use the project's own Playwright: import it from
your project (for example `import { chromium } from "playwright"`) or run the
project-local CLI with `npx playwright`. There is no global Playwright on
`PATH`; it lives in your project's `node_modules` like any other dependency.
Launch Chromium with the `--no-sandbox` argument, since this container has no
Chromium sandbox and a default launch fails.

## The showcase

The game ships with its store-page presentation: a `showcase/` directory at the
project root holding a player-facing description of the game and a short
carousel of media captured from the running game. `specs/showcase.md` defines
its contents — the description, the `showcase.toml` carousel, and the media it
names.

## Commit your work

Your project root is a git repository, initialized before you started with the
starter files in its first commit. Your finished implementation is published
directly from this repository's commits, so work you leave uncommitted is not
published. When the build is complete, commit everything: run `git add -A` then
`git commit`, and confirm `git status` reports a clean working tree with nothing
uncommitted or untracked. A `.gitignore` already excludes `node_modules/`, the
build output, and coverage reports, so a clean tree means committing your
source, your tests, your produced files under `assets/`, your `showcase/`
directory, your `package-lock.json`, and your `README.md`.
